sitting in court as he's handed a  12-year jail sentence the scene is a far  cry from his former life of luxury  the son of mozambique's former president  was among 11 people found guilty in a  corruption case known as the tuna Bond  scandal  it involves state-owned companies  illicitly borrowing hundreds of millions  of dollars from International Banks to  fund a tuna fishing operation and  surveillance vessels  but the cash disappeared and  mozambique's government hid the  borrowing from both Parliament and the  public  when the full scale of the Hidden debt  came to light in 2016 it triggered an  economic crisis in Mozambique  the country's currency lost a third of  its value inflation surged and foreign  donors pulled out  former president Amando gabuza who was  in office when The loans were agreed  testified at the trial his son was found  guilty of influencing his father to get  the government to approve the deals  and of using a 33 million dollar bribe  to buy luxury cars and a mansion in  South Africa eight of the 19 defendants  were acquitted while the rest were given  prison terms ranging between 10 and 12  years  handing down the sentences the judge  said the scam had plunged thousands of  people into poverty across the country  many outside the court said the Judgment  sent a clear message  to those who are still willing to commit  such crimes that there's no it is no  longer  and the country  where you can come in such crime and and  walk free  so I think that those who orchestrated  this  crime will not go free  as  we used to see in this country back in  the days  the debt Scandal exposed corruption on a  global scale and sparked legal cases  across three continents given Calvert Al  Jazeera
 

